{"step_index":258,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T18:24:36Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nits working in claude man if you want to trim trim it  hermese agent guide [Quickstart](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart#quickstart)\nConnect NexLev MCP to your preferred AI assistant and start analyzing YouTube channels in under 5 minutes. Choose the platform you use below.\n[Prerequisites](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart#prerequisites)\nBefore you begin, make sure you have:\nActive NexLev account — Free for all users. [Upgrade to Pro](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart?required_nexlev_lite_pro_access=true) for higher quota limits.\nClaudeChatGPTOpenClawHermes\n[Via Hermes Agent](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart#via-hermes-agent)\nHow it works. Hermes is a self-hosted autonomous agent that connects natively to NexLev as an MCP server — no intermediary CLI required. The flow is: You (messaging app) → Hermes Gateway → NexLev MCP tools\nUnlike the OpenClaw path (which routes through Claude Code / Codex CLI), Hermes talks to MCP servers directly via its built-in MCP client.\nPrerequisites — Hermes installed\nmacOS / Windows (recommended): Download the Hermes Desktop installer from [hermes-agent.nousresearch.com](https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/) — it installs both the desktop app and the CLI in one step.\nLinux / macOS / WSL2 (CLI only):\ncurl -fsSL https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/install.sh | bashsource ~/.bashrc # or source ~/.zshrc\nCopy\nStep 1 — Run first-time setup\nhermes setup\nCopy\nWalks you through choosing a model provider (Nous Portal, OpenRouter, Anthropic, etc.). If you already have ~/.openclaw, the setup wizard detects it and offers to migrate your settings, memories, and API keys automatically.\nStep 2 — Register NexLev as an MCP server\nAdd NexLev to ~/.hermes/config.yaml:\nmcp_servers: nexlev: url: \"https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp\" auth: oauth\nCopy\nVerify it was saved:\nhermes mcp list\nCopy\nTest the connection:\nhermes mcp test nexlev\nCopy\nStep 3 — Authenticate NexLev\nOn first connection, run:\nhermes \n<truncated 10275 bytes>\nkstart#keyword-research)\nFind high-volume, low-competition keywords for cooking content\nCopy\n[Content Strategy](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart#content-strategy)\nWhat content strategy should I use for a tech review channel with 10K subscribers?\nCopy\n[Competitive Research](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart#competitive-research)\nCompare successful channels in the fitness niche and identify common patterns\nCopy\n[Thumbnail Generation](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart#thumbnail-generation)\nGenerate a thumbnail for my video \"I Tried Waking Up at 4AM for 30 Days\" — match the style of the top productivity channels\nCopy\n[Off-Platform Earnings](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart#off-platform-earnings)\nWhat does @AliAbdaal sell outside of ads, and which brands sponsor the most finance channels?\nCopy\n[Network Research](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart#network-research)\nWhich network is MrBeast signed to, and what other channels are under it?\nCopy\n[Understanding NexLev Responses](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/docs/mcp/quickstart#understanding-nexlev-responses)\nWhen you use NexLev through Claude, ChatGPT, or Claude Code CLI, you'll receive interactive widgets displaying:\nChannel data — Comprehensive metrics, performance indicators, and growth analytics\nSortable results — Click column headers to sort by different metrics\nExpandable details — View in-depth information for each result\nFiltering options — Refine results by various criteria\nPagination — Navigate through large result sets                                          now just for refreance i gave you read and understand how to do it and do if fast man \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-08-25T23:54:36+05:30.\n\nThe user has uploaded 1 image(s):\n- C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/antigravity/brain/9ecf2b20-4f14-47c7-887d-090725d6fd1c/.user_uploaded/media_1787682026991.png\nYou can embed this image in an artifact if you need the USER to review it.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":259,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T18:24:36Z","content":"I see it working in your Claude.ai! And I notice NexLev tools are actually **already registered** in Antigravity! Let me try calling them directly:","thinking":"Now I understand! Looking at the screenshot, the user has NexLev MCP working in Claude.ai web — it shows \"Get batch channel metrics status\" with the NexLev icon. \n\nThe key insight from the docs: ALL paths (Claude, Hermes, OpenClaw) require OAuth authentication. There's no way around it. The authentication happens through the browser OAuth flow.\n\nFor Hermes: `hermes mcp login nexlev` → browser opens → login → tokens cached at `~/.hermes/mcp-tokens/nexlev.json`\nFor OpenClaw: auth inherited from Claude Code CLI → `/mcp` → authenticate in browser\nFor Claude Code itself, the flow is similar — running `/mcp`, selecting nexlev, and authenticating opens the browser, with tokens cached for silent reuse afterward. The user already has this working in Claude.ai web, but that token lives server-side at Anthropic, not locally. Re-reading NexLev's error message, it accepts either an OAuth Bearer token or an API key passed via Authorization header, x-api-key header, or api_key query parameter — so there may be an alternate path using an API key instead of going through the full OAuth dance.\n\nNexLev's \"no API key needed\" marketing actually just refers to the OAuth flow being keyless for end users, not that API keys don't exist — the user might have one they're unaware of. For our bridge without a browser-based session, we need either the OAuth token or that API key. The real blocker has been that our Python OAuth script opens the browser fine, but the redirect back to our local callback isn't completing — the user logs in and lands on the NexLev dashboard instead of bouncing back to us, possibly because auth.nexlev.io first redirects through Google before coming back.\n\nI'm now considering