{"step_index":273,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T18:31:38Z","content":"You're right, I'm sorry. The MCP is FREE and works in Claude.ai without any API key — it uses OAuth. Let me do the OAuth through Claude Code CLI right now. I'll run it interactively:","thinking":"The user is very frustrated. Let me understand the situation:\n\n1. The NexLev MCP works in Claude.ai web (screenshot shows it working)\n2. In Claude.ai web, the OAuth was handled automatically by Anthropic's servers when the user connected it via the \"Connectors\" page\n3. The user wants the same NexLev MCP to work here in Antigravity\n4. The NexLev API key is PAID (the \"NexLev API\" section requires a paid plan)\n5. The MCP itself is FREE for all accounts - it uses OAuth, not API keys\n\nThe key issue: Claude.ai handled the OAuth flow server-side. Antigravity needs to handle it client-side, which requires the browser auth step.\nI found the credentials file showing the access token is empty, so the CLI never completed auth. The user says no approval page appears when the browser opens, which makes me suspect that since they're already logged into Google, the authorize request auto-redirects straight back to the NexLev dashboard instead of hitting my localhost callback - meaning the redirect URI configuration on NexLev's side might not match what my script expects.\n\nLet me try the Claude Code CLI approach instead, since it should handle the OAuth flow including PKCE and redirects correctly. I'll try running it interactively and automating the `/mcp` command, or look into whether there's a direct CLI auth trigger like `hermes mcp login nexlev` that caches tokens locally.","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"claude 2>&1\"","Cwd":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\renu5\"","IsDaemon":"true","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Starting Claude Code\"","toolSummary":"\"Claude interactive mode\""}}]}
